{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And it came to pass, when Jacob saw.</b> In this passage Scripture mentions 'his mother's brother' several times, to make known that all the effort Jacob expended for the sheep to water them, and the compassion he showed toward Rachel the daughter of Laban, he did not do for Laban's honor but only for the honor of his mother, because he was his mother's brother. And because of this, every time the text mentions the name of Laban the wicked, it mentions 'his mother's brother' each time, to make clear that he watered Laban's sheep only because he was his mother's brother, and he called to mind his mother, whose love had moved her to give him this counsel to go to him:" "<b>And furthermore,</b> there is another reason here: for when a person sees or hears something he desires, this is a cause for an increase of strength in his natural powers. And since one might think that Jacob's nature, upon seeing Rachel, was drawn after physical desire, and that his strength increased in rolling the stone beyond what he could normally do, Scripture therefore needed to say 'his mother's brother' several times, to indicate that no increase of strength in his nature came from love for Rachel, and her sight stirred in him no arousal such as is natural for other men — but everything was for love of his mother. And because of this, Scripture mentions Rachel and the sheep before it mentions 'and he rolled the stone,' and did not say: 'And it came to pass when Jacob saw Rachel the daughter of Laban his mother's brother, and he rolled the stone':" "<b>And Jacob drew near, and rolled the stone from the mouth of the well.</b> Jacob was great in strength and might, for the stone which the shepherds of three flocks of sheep could not move from its place — he alone rolled it from the mouth of the well. And this despite his being weary from the journey he had traveled, and also from the Torah, which is called תושיה ('resourceful knowledge') because it wearies the strength of a man who has studied until now in the house of Eber — for it is well known that when he departed from the house of his father Isaac he did not go immediately to Haran, to the house of Laban, but went to study Torah in the house of Eber, and remained there fourteen (י״ד) years. And although this is not mentioned in the text, there is no proof-text for this matter but there is a remembrance of the matter (אין ראיה לדבר זכר לדבר) — for thus we find that his father commanded him when he left: ״קום לך פדנה ארם וקח לך משם אשה״, and he understood in that command, beneath the expression 'wife,' a hidden meaning, namely that the Torah is likenened to a woman, and concerning her Solomon, peace be upon him, wrote in the proverbs of Mishlei (Proverbs 31:10): ״אשת חיל מי ימצא״, and he said (Proverbs 12:4): ״אשת חיל עטרת בעלה״. And the Sages, of blessed memory, said: do not read מורשה ('inheritance') but מאורשה ('betrothed'). Therefore he first pursued the hidden matter and went to serve in the house of Eber fourteen (י״ד) years. And behold, the Sages, of blessed memory (רז״ל), who are recipients of the truth, received this and informed us of the matter according to the reckoning of the years of Jacob's life from the years of Ishmael, as they mentioned in tractate Megillah (17a): when Jacob was blessed by his father he was sixty-three (ס״ג) years old, and this is the passage of his leaving his father's house, as it is written: ״וישמע יעקב אל אביו ואל אמו״. And it is taught: fourteen (י״ד) years Jacob was hidden and served in the house of Eber, and when he stood at the well he was seventy-seven (ע״ז) years old. According to this, before he went to Laban he studied fourteen (י״ד) years in the house of Eber, even though this is not mentioned in the text."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 29, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:17.270286716Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}